00:01:46,792 --> 00:01:47,792
this setting here

44
00:01:48,792 --> 00:01:51,116
to support instruction files,

45
00:01:51,778 --> 00:01:53,327
which allows you to add a

46
00:01:53,327 --> 00:01:58,527
copilot-instructions.md file so a Markdown

47
00:01:58,656 --> 00:02:00,230
file with that specific name

48
00:02:00,808 --> 00:02:03,217
in a.github folder in your project.

49
00:02:03,956 --> 00:02:06,863
And you could put your own instructions,

50
00:02:06,927 --> 00:02:09,497
which should be short and precise, into that

51
00:02:09,497 --> 00:02:09,689
file.

52
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:12,709
And if you then enable this setting, GitHub

53
00:02:12,709 --> 00:02:15,712
Copilot would take these instructions into

54
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:19,149
account whenever it generates code through

55
00:02:19,278 --> 00:02:20,835
its chat functionality.

56
00:02:22,104 --> 00:02:24,048
So you could, for example, describe a

57
00:02:24,048 --> 00:02:26,890
certain code style you wanna enforce. In

58
00:02:27,002 --> 00:02:28,381
such an instructions file.

59
00:02:29,551 --> 00:02:32,132
That is not something I'm using in all my

60
00:02:32,132 --> 00:02:34,424
projects, but it is something that can

61
00:02:34,520 --> 00:02:37,342
definitely be helpful in some projects as it

62
00:02:37,342 --> 00:02:41,077
can help ensure that the code suggestions

63
00:02:41,590 --> 00:02:44,764
and the code snippets generated by Copilot

64
00:02:45,261 --> 00:02:48,899
are correct immediately or at least a bit

65
00:02:48,899 --> 00:02:51,416
sooner than they otherwise might be. So

66
00:02:51,528 --> 00:02:53,805
putting those instructions into this file

67
00:02:54,318 --> 00:02:57,107
might save you some extra iteration cycles

68
00:02:57,363 --> 00:02:59,994
where you tell Copilot to change the code.

69
00:03:00,491 --> 00:03:02,241
That's the idea here. By putting these

70
00:03:02,257 --> 00:03:04,263
instructions into this file, you have a

71
00:03:04,263 --> 00:03:06,590
higher chance of getting suggestions that

72
00:03:07,152 --> 00:03:09,030
are the way you want them right from the

73
00:03:09,030 --> 00:03:09,495
start.

74
00:03:10,218 --> 00:03:12,930
So that is one setting you should consider

75
00:03:13,010 --> 00:03:15,787
enabling. Then you have to add this file

76
00:03:16,381 --> 00:03:18,997
in this specific.github folder, put your

77
00:03:19,029 --> 00:03:21,452
instructions into that file, keep them short

78
00:03:21,452 --> 00:03:22,174
and precise,

79
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:25,032
and see how that affects the code

80
00:03:25,064 --> 00:03:27,956
generations created by GitHub Copilot.

81
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:31,057
It is worth noting, as mentioned here, that

82
00:03:31,154 --> 00:03:33,982
poor instructions can actually lead to worse

83
00:03:33,982 --> 00:03:36,139
results than having no instructions at all,

84
00:03:36,982 --> 00:03:39,252
which is why, again, I'm not using this

85
00:03:39,252 --> 00:03:41,295
feature all the time, but it is worth a

86
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:42,009
closer look.

87
00:03:43,590 --> 00:03:46,749
Now, another very useful or very interesting

88
00:03:46,927 --> 00:03:48,612
feature in my opinion

89
00:03:49,276 --> 00:03:52,729
is this Temporal Context feature here.

90
00:03:53,772 --> 00:03:57,191
This feature, when enabled, ensures that

91
00:03:57,287 --> 00:04:00,641
GitHub Copilot automatically takes the last

92
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:04,685
edited files and the code it saw right

93
00:04:04,685 --> 00:04:08,328
before you start asking a question into

94
00:04:08,377 --> 00:04:11,490
account. So if you just worked in file A and

95
00:04:11,570 --> 00:04:11,859
you then

96
00:04:12,549 --> 00:04:16,016
try to use GitHub Copilot Chat in file B,

97
00:04:16,786 --> 00:04:18,727
any code you might have in file A

98
00:04:19,352 --> 00:04:22,159
should hopefully be taken into account for

99
00:04:22,255 --> 00:04:25,030
generating the answer for your question you

100
00:04:25,062 --> 00:04:27,709
might have asked in file B, so in another

101
00:04:27,709 --> 00:04:31,253
file. So for example, if file A exports a

102
00:04:31,301 --> 00:04:34,477
certain function and a good answer for your

103
00:04:34,589 --> 00:04:36,354
question in file B

104
00:04:36,931 --> 00:04:39,064
uses this function, if that's the case,

105
00:04:39,497 --> 00:04:41,871
GitHub Copilot hopefully takes its knowledge

106
00:04:42,064 --> 00:04:45,143
of what it saw in file A and suggests

107
00:04:45,352 --> 00:04:47,566
importing that function and using that

108
00:04:47,598 --> 00:04:50,054
function. So it simply helps with working

109
00:04:50,632 --> 00:04:53,151
with code that's split across multiple

110
00:04:53,216 --> 00:04:56,425
files, for example. This temporal context

111
00:04:56,425 --> 00:04:58,656
feature can be very useful for that.

